WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Hello and welcome to this episode,&nbsp;
where we are going to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.840
the European Union’s proposal for&nbsp;
an Artificial Intelligence act.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:16.980
It was proposed by the European Commission&nbsp;
on the 21st of April 2021 and very recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:24.660
on the 14th of June this year, the European&nbsp;
parliament has voted on it. The whole AI act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:31.020
is a document of almost 90 pages. It also comes&nbsp;
with a 10 pages summary, see the links in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.540
description below. In this video, we summarize&nbsp;
and break down the most important points for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:42.000
you and tell you why this is important&nbsp;
for you even if you’re not an EU citizen.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.780
But not before we thank Assembly AI, the sponsor&nbsp;
of today’s video! AssemblyAI offers superhuman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:55.560
AI models for speech recognition, automatic&nbsp;
transcription, speech summarization, and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:02.760
through a secure and scalable API. As an applied&nbsp;
AI company, AssemblyAI’s mission is to empower app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.880
builders to build 10 times faster, focus on their&nbsp;
specific use cases and user needs, and win market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.940
share with a true technology partner. Last week,&nbsp;
AssemblyAI announced its new Conformer-2 model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:23.280
the company’s latest AI model for automatic speech&nbsp;
recognition that was trained on 1.1 million hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.600
of audio data. Now this week, AssemblyAI is&nbsp;
launching LeMUR, the easiest way to build LLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:37.440
apps on spoken data. Users can search, summarize,&nbsp;
ask questions, and generate new text, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.620
knowledge of all an application’s spoken data.&nbsp;
LeMUR performs intelligent retrieval to offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:49.980
high-quality LLM responses with a single API call.&nbsp;
You can play around with both Conformer-1 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:55.680
LeMUR in their free AI playground with the link&nbsp;
in the description below. Now, back to the video.&nbsp;

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:03.600
The EU has a draft on the AI act, so it is not&nbsp;
yet enforced, as it is still to be approved and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:10.440
adopted by the member states. So, why did the EU&nbsp;
feel like there is need of an AI act? Because on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.660
the one hand, AI can benefit society by&nbsp;
helping science, and improving health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:21.600
by finding new drugs for example, it can help us&nbsp;
with protecting the environment, it can help in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:27.900
transport by optimizing bus routes and estimated&nbsp;
time of arrivals, it can help feed the population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:34.200
by optimizing agriculture. And the list of the&nbsp;
benefits can go on and on. But on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.200
AI can also have some questionable uses. For&nbsp;
example, it can help social media companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:48.180
make more money because AI algorithms help them&nbsp;
make you stay engaged on their platforms. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:54.000
even worse, AI is a double-edged sword that can be&nbsp;
used in even more concerning applications such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.480
face recognition at scale, social scoring and many&nbsp;
other applications that empower discrimination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.580
cut on the freedom of expression, and&nbsp;
can harm data protection and privacy.&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:14.100
So, the aim of the EU AI act is to regulate AI&nbsp;
to protect its citizens from harm enabled by AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:20.880
while still fostering AI innovation for good&nbsp;
applications. It is important to understand here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:28.560
that this AI Act is by design a product safety&nbsp;
regulation, think CE label on electronics, safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.260
standards for personal protection equipment and&nbsp;
so on. It is not intended to slow technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:41.520
advancements, but rather to make sure that AI&nbsp;
products on the market don’t harm anyone. The EU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:47.640
wants to step in on AI matters especially since so&nbsp;
far, authorities cannot step when it comes to uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:56.100
AI. An EU-wide regulation would avoid fragmented,&nbsp;
country-wide measures that would make using an AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:02.820
produced and regulated in an EU country difficult&nbsp;
in another one. A clear stance of governments on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:08.880
AI would also make the current situation better in&nbsp;
which the legal uncertainty discourages businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:15.300
to use and rely on AI systems. And I think it’s&nbsp;
understandable that I am a bit dissuaded to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:20.580
AI generated pictures in my videos because&nbsp;
who knows if following a new law, I might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:26.700
banned in the future from showing my videos with&nbsp;
such content in the US or another country. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:32.640
world so far has been quite slow in making laws&nbsp;
about AI. Much slower than the technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.640
progress. For example, the United States were&nbsp;
very hands-off in regulating AI so far. Who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:46.140
knows whether this might change with the recent&nbsp;
senate hearings. This AI act draft makes the EU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:53.520
the first to have a serious regulatory proposal&nbsp;
on AI. Then, let’s see what the AI act contains.&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:05:00.300
To whom does this regulation apply to? We&nbsp;
cite “The new rules would apply primarily to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:06.000
providers of AI systems established within&nbsp;
the EU or in a third country placing AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.660
systems on the EU market or putting them into&nbsp;
service in the EU, as well as to users of AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:18.750
systems located in the EU.” Interestingly,&nbsp;
it does not apply for military purposes. Aha.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:25.200
But what does even count as AI according to&nbsp;
the EU? We know that the definition of AI is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:33.000
very debated, even by experts. In this draft, AI&nbsp;
is, we quote: “...software that is developed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.300
[specific] techniques and approaches [listed in&nbsp;
Annex 1] and can, for a given set of human-defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:45.180
objectives, generate outputs such as content,&nbsp;
predictions, recommendations, or decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:52.380
influencing the environments they interact with.”&nbsp;
End quote. Hm, do you agree with this definition?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.680
There are stakeholders who think this definition&nbsp;
is too broad. And they do have a point since this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:03.900
definition even applies to a simple search through&nbsp;
the internet if powered by a neural network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:10.740
Should that be regulated too? Ms. Coffee bean’s&nbsp;
take here is the following: It does not matter how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:16.500
AI is implemented, but its application. She does&nbsp;
not care what the technology or algorithm behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.240
a task is, but the task itself. Like, should&nbsp;
we care whether face recognition is implemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:27.120
by the latest in machine learning, such as deep&nbsp;
neural networks or if it is a very complicated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.580
lengthy if-else algorithm? It is the application&nbsp;
here that matters. The problem that advancements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:38.820
in AI have brought is that before, we could not&nbsp;
do face recognition or deepfakes at scale and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:44.700
now we can. If the definition of AI is narrow and&nbsp;
only includes deep neural networks, then one could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:51.660
circumvent it. How? With an if-else program.&nbsp;
Ah, but there is no super complicated if-else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:57.420
program that does face recognition, but what if I&nbsp;
take a neural network that does face recognition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:03.540
use another neural network to transcribe me the&nbsp;
patterns of the first network into a humanly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:10.080
impossible lengthy to write if-else algorithm?&nbsp;
So, in my opinion, not the technology, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:16.080
applications should be regulated. And we’ll see,&nbsp;
this is the way which the EU follows as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:21.780
where not the technology, but the task it&nbsp;
solves is the one posed under scrutiny.&nbsp;

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:28.320
So, how does it to it, to regulate this software?&nbsp;
Well, by distinguishing between AI systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.920
posing (i) unacceptable risk, (ii) high risk,&nbsp;
(iii) limited risk, and (iv) low or minimal risk.&nbsp;

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:41.760
Unacceptable risk would be prohibited because it&nbsp;
is “considered to be a clear threat to people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:48.480
safety, livelihoods and rights.” Such unacceptable&nbsp;
Risk AI would use manipulative ‘subliminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.180
techniques’ (curious what that means) or would&nbsp;
be exploiting vulnerable groups (with physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.180 --> 00:08:00.120
or mental disability). Social scoring is also&nbsp;
unacceptable or biometric identification such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:06.120
as face recognition in public spaces. But there&nbsp;
are exceptions: In cases like serious crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:12.180
if an EU arrest warrant has been issued, remote&nbsp;
biometric identification systems may be used.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:19.140
As for high risk systems, they create adverse&nbsp;
impact, such as  Biometric identification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:23.460
and categorisation of natural persons;&nbsp;
 Management and operation of critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.180
infrastructure;  Education and&nbsp;
vocational training;  Employment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:33.120
worker management and access to self-employment;&nbsp;
 Access to and enjoyment of essential private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.320
services and public services and benefits;&nbsp;
 Law enforcement;  Migration, asylum and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:38.700
border control management;  Administration&nbsp;
of justice and democratic processes. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:45.960
high-risk systems would be following new rules:&nbsp;
they will need to receive a CE registration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:53.400
just like all electronics, toys, personal safety&nbsp;
equipment and so on, that are sold on the European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:58.320
market. For that they need to conform to&nbsp;
the safety legislation of their own field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.720
(just like medical devices are) and to another&nbsp;
range of requirements such as risk management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:10.440
testing, technical robustness, data training and&nbsp;
data governance, transparency, human oversight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:16.860
and cybersecurity, all checked by a third party&nbsp;
before being placed on the market or put to use.&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:25.080
As for AI of “limited risk”, these are systems&nbsp;
that, we cite “interact with humans (i.e.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:29.580
chatbots), emotion recognition systems,&nbsp;
biometric categorisation systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:35.700
and AI systems that generate or manipulate&nbsp;
image, audio, or video content (i.e.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:41.040
deepfakes) [They] would be subject to a&nbsp;
set of transparency obligations”. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.240
requirements include description of&nbsp;
data sources for training the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:50.820
inclusion of standardised benchmark scores&nbsp;
and disclosure of machine generated content.&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:57.840
On a first glance these obligations maybe seem&nbsp;
reasonably easy to comply with, but none of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:03.060
current foundation models, from GPT-4, Stable&nbsp;
Diffusion to even Luminous, which was created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:08.160
as a more “EU friendly” model, could comply&nbsp;
with these obligations if they were in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:13.440
today. This study from Stanford University’s&nbsp;
Center for Research on Foundation Models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.440
https://crfm.stanford.edu/2023/06/15/eu-ai-act.html&nbsp;
shows that for example, almost all models except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:22.320
for GPT-NeoX or BLOOM, use copyrighted data&nbsp;
for training or did not disclose that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:28.080
did not use copyrighted data. Very few models&nbsp;
disclose the compute and energy requirements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:32.700
their data sources or conduct&nbsp;
extensive evaluations or testing.&nbsp;

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:38.160
And lastly, for low or minimal risk&nbsp;
AI, the EU imposes no obligations.&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:44.400
There will be AI supervisory and surveillance&nbsp;
authorities at national level to supervise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.380
the implementation of the regulation and&nbsp;
administer fines depending on the severity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:55.860
of the infringement (up to €30 million or 6&nbsp;
% of the total worldwide annual turnover).&nbsp;

00:10:55.860 --> 00:11:01.800
To foster developments and encourage startups,&nbsp;
the Commision proposes regulatory sandboxing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:06.840
which is, we cite “a controlled environment&nbsp;
that facilitates the development, testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:12.780
and validation of innovative AI systems (for a&nbsp;
limited period of time) before they are put on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:18.360
market […] . Sandboxing will enable participants&nbsp;
to use personal data to foster AI innovation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:24.840
without prejudice to the GDPR requirements.” End&nbsp;
quote. Since none of this is implemented yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:29.640
it is easy to feel unsecure about how this&nbsp;
will develop, since we do not know yet if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.620
every nation will be able to assemble&nbsp;
a worthy board of experts for this.&nbsp;

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:41.160
Will the AI act also apply to research? We&nbsp;
got to ask this question in our interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.560
with the EU parliament members in charge of&nbsp;
the AI act themselves, and the answer is no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:52.140
this act will not really affect researchers. As&nbsp;
long as AI is developed and released as research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:57.180
and not as a product, it does not fall under&nbsp;
this regulation. So, a website with a model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:02.280
demo from research or code to it should be fine.&nbsp;
But when a company is making money by making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:07.620
it a product, it falls under the regulation.
If you’re using AI tools yourself, for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:12.960
to generate images or by getting help from ChatGPT&nbsp;
to write text, these regulations will likely not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:18.300
affect you very much. As this regulation has&nbsp;
its basis in product safety regulation, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:24.060
major focus of it is to make sure companies don’t&nbsp;
put products on the market that impose a danger on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:29.820
consumers. But as an individual, you are free to&nbsp;
use the products that are certified as you wish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:35.220
What you might see are watermarks to indicate&nbsp;
AI generated content as such, which come as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:41.220
part of the transparency regulations for limited&nbsp;
risk AI systems. Of course, if you want to use an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:46.800
AI system that the EU deemed unsafe to use, you&nbsp;
will not be able to get access to it in Europe.&nbsp;

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:53.040
I know, many of you are not from the EU, so you&nbsp;
might think that this does not really affect you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:57.480
But the past has shown that regulations&nbsp;
that apply to the European market often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:02.760
affect the global market as well, since a lot of&nbsp;
producers sell to Europe and must conform to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.820
standards. This is known as the Brussels effect:&nbsp;
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_effect&nbsp;

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:10.980
Just like with the California effect in the US,&nbsp;
companies that try to sell their products on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.960
global market will often try to comply with&nbsp;
the most stringent standard available. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:19.920
way they won’t have to design different&nbsp;
products for different markets and will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:25.020
still be able to export to the whole world.
This was already the case for the production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:30.900
of chemicals, airplane emissions and more recently&nbsp;
data protection, where now major US tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:35.940
implemented the European GDPR compliance for&nbsp;
all their customers, even outside of the EU.&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:42.600
And this was our summary about the AI act. What&nbsp;
are your thoughts on it? It is a good start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:48.120
but we will see how it develops. As stated by Mr.&nbsp;
Tudorache in our interview, it will take a while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:53.940
until the law will come into effect, maybe&nbsp;
we will need to wait until 2026. But surely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:58.380
companies that want to become future proof&nbsp;
may want to already start complying with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:03.060
these new rules. In fact, while preparing&nbsp;
this video, seven leading AI companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:08.280
in the US have signed an agreement to put&nbsp;
some safeguards on their products. For now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:13.620
this is just voluntary self-regulation, but it&nbsp;
is a first step. If you want to hear first-hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:18.060
from the regulators, check out our interview&nbsp;
on the channel of the European Parliament.&nbsp;

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:25.680
We hoped you liked this video and don’t forget&nbsp;
to subscribe to also see our next one. Okay, bye!

